  "body": "**What happens.** A whole-model 3D Pocket clears a notch that is open to the part's outside: a quadrant step in the side of a block. It leaves tall ridges of stock along the part's outer edge at the notch, because the cutter stays inside the model boundary. The op reports `ok`. The next setup then refuses the post, correctly: `fixture '…' stands inside the stock carried from setup 'Setup 1' (9.93 mm deep)`.\n\n**Numbers (CamCraft v0.20.0, a user's two-sided part).**\n- The part is a 30 mm cube made of two L halves, with R3.125 fillets. Setup 1 cuts the top notch with a 3D Pocket: whole model, shell on, offset rings, a 6.35 mm flat end mill, 2.032 mm stepover, 0.15 mm stepdown, 0 stock to leave. The floor is Z 19.826.\n- In the middle of the notch, the carried stock is cut to the floor, as expected.\n- Near the notch's open edges (for example X 14.81 at the part edge), the carried stock stays at Z 33.326 and 34.676, about 14-15 mm above the floor. The finished model there is at about Z 18-19.\n- The flip into Setup 2 and the fixture positions are correct. The fixtures clear the finished model by 1.0 to 3.6 mm. The carried-stock check measures the uncut ridge correctly.\n\n**Expected.** Either the pocket clears an open-sided notch to the part's outer edge (the open side is air, so the cutter may go past the model boundary into the stock), or the op says by name that it leaves material along an open edge, and how much.\n\n**Workaround today.** A machining boundary or a stock-source region that reaches past the part's edge at the open sides. Not yet tested.\n\n**Done means:** a 3D Pocket on a block with an open-sided quadrant notch leaves no material above the floor plus the stock to leave at the open edges, pinned by a test. Or the op names the material it leaves.\n\nFound while making a two-sided job (soft jaws in Setup 2). The user's files stay private.\n",
